	'st_h' => 'How a layout is tested on site',
	'st_h3' => 'Half a day, at full scale',
	'st_more' => 'The site test',
	'st_p' => 'We tape the floor where every bench, range and door will stand. Your brigade walks a lunch service with empty pans, and Piotr moves the tape until nobody waits, crosses or reaches.',
	'stp_a1' => 'Yes. The site test requires a bare, unobstructed floor shell so Piotr Nowak can apply coloured tape accurately across the entire room. If you are refurbishing an active kitchen, the test is scheduled either before new equipment is delivered or during your planned strip-out phase. Having an open floor allows us to outline bench footprints, swing doors, and trolley lanes without physical interference, giving your brigade a realistic simulation of the future space.',
	'stp_a2' => 'We require your head chef and core line cooks to attend the walking session starting at 14:00. Having the cooks who will actually work the line is essential because their reach, physical height, and handedness determine whether an aisle feels comfortable. Restaurant owners and general managers are encouraged to observe, while Piotr Nowak guides the brigade through menu ticket movements and notes every required dimensional adjustment.',
	'stp_a3' => 'No. We conduct floor tests exclusively during quiet hours or dark days. For operating kitchens undergoing redesign, we run the session from 13:00 to 17:00 between morning breakfast and evening service. Because we only use rolls of tape, steel measures, paper plans, and empty cookware, there is no construction noise, dust, or contractor mess, allowing your kitchen team to prepare and complete evening dinner service without operational disruption.',
	'stp_a4' => 'If your culinary concept changes, we adapt the tape lines during the test. Introducing a plancha, adding deep fryers, or shifting to cold larder prep can be mocked up on the bare floor immediately. Because adjustments are tested live with your cooks before drawings are finalised, we can refine workstation widths and canopy lengths instantly, avoiding costly redesign fees, contractor variations, or equipment order cancellations later.',
	'stp_b1' => 'Paper drawings and screen models cannot show how three cooks feel when turning from a hot range with heavy pans. Architectural blueprints look spacious until you factor in open oven doors, low-boy refrigerator drawers, and trolley movements during service peaks. We tape the complete kitchen layout at full scale on your bare floor before anyone orders a single piece of equipment. Ordering ranges, canopies, and stainless benches from manufacturer catalogues without testing clearances on site is the single most expensive mistake a restaurant can make. Once gas pipes and floor drains are cast into concrete, moving a pass even fifty centimetres requires jackhammers, lost trading weeks, and substantial contractor variation costs. By spending half a day walking the real floor with empty pans, your brigade proves every station, aisle, and turn before your contractor orders equipment or begins building.',
	'stp_b2' => 'Piotr Nowak leads the four-hour floor test on site following a structured schedule. At 13:00 Piotr tapes the floor, laying out every bench, range, sink, and door swing at full scale using coloured tape. At 14:00 your head chef and line brigade arrive to walk a simulated service with empty pans and plates. At 15:30 we adjust the layout, moving tape lines until cooks no longer cross paths or wait for stations. At 16:30 we record final photographs and dimensional notes for drawing updates.',
	'stp_b3' => 'The test operates through three clear stages. First is Tape: coloured tape marks out every bench, range, pass, and trolley route at full size on your bare floor. Second is Walk: your own cooks walk a lunch service with empty pans, timing each movement from prep station to pass. Third is Adjust: Piotr Nowak moves the tape until nobody waits, crosses awkwardly, or reaches unsafely. All adjustments go back into the layout drawings that same week.',
	'stp_cap1' => 'Half a day, at full scale',
	'stp_cap2' => 'The layout taped on your floor, a service walked with empty pans.',
